  1	//! [`CAllocator`]: the standard [`Allocator`] over a C allocation family.
  2	//!
  3	//! A [`CLenAlloc`] policy is a C allocation family: `malloc` and the
  4	//! alignment it vouches for ([`c_alloc_len`](CLenAlloc::c_alloc_len),
  5	//! [`ALIGN`](CLenAlloc::ALIGN)), `realloc`
  6	//! ([`c_realloc_len`](CLenAlloc::c_realloc_len)) and `free`
  7	//! ([`c_drop_len`](crate::CLenDrop::c_drop_len)), declared once with
  8	//! [`impl_clendrop!`](crate::impl_clendrop) and
  9	//! [`impl_clenalloc!`](crate::impl_clenalloc). `CAllocator<P>` is that family
 10	//! as the standard `Allocator`, so the standard owners hold memory C frees:
 11	//!
 12	//! - `Box<T, CAllocator<P>>` and `Vec<T, CAllocator<P>>` (`alloc` feature)
 13	//!   allocate, grow and free through the family, and adopt what C allocated
 14	//!   with it;
 15	//! - `CBox<Foo, CAllocator<P>>`, `CStrBox<CAllocator<P>>` and
 16	//!   `CVec<T, CAllocator<P>>` take it as their policy, a storage-only one,
 17	//!   and convert to and from the standard owners without copying.
 18	//!
 19	//! It is the C counterpart of [`RustAlloc`](crate::RustAlloc), which offers
 20	//! Rust's allocator to the owners: replacing one with the other changes the
 21	//! owner's policy and nothing its consumers write.
 22	
 23	use core::alloc::{AllocError, Allocator, Layout};
 24	use core::ffi::{CStr, c_char};
 25	use core::mem::MaybeUninit;
 26	use core::ptr::NonNull;
 27	
 28	#[cfg(feature = "alloc")]
 29	use crate::refs::{CBox, CVec};
 30	#[cfg(feature = "alloc")]
 31	use crate::traits::CSliceElem;
 32	use crate::traits::{
 33	    CAlloc, CAllocZeroed, CCell, CDrop, CDupClone, CLenAlloc, CLenClone, CLenDrop, CNew, CZeroable,
 34	};
